<Base title="Research Program · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="Language, reasoning, and human and artificial cognition: from philosophy and language learning to behavioural experiments and mechanistic interpretability.">
  <article class="rp">
    <h1 class="rp-h1">Research Program</h1>
    <p class="rp-lead">{identityLong}</p>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">How I came to these questions</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        My undergraduate studies in philosophy drew me to logic: a way to make the structure of
        an argument explicit and examine what follows from it. In my thesis, I studied Leibniz’s
        logical system and his proposal for a universal symbolic language. This raised a question
        that continues to interest me: how do formal descriptions of meaning relate to the ways
        people actually understand and reason?
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Learning languages gave that question an everyday setting. I found myself paying attention
        to how sentence structure contributes to meaning, and how different languages can express
        similar ideas through different structures. This led me to ask when differences in linguistic
        form affect understanding and thought. Alongside philosophy, I studied psychology and took
        part in a project on children’s learning difficulties. My master’s studies in Human and
        Artificial Intelligence have brought these interests into experimental and computational work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Understanding human cognition</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        I am interested in how people learn concepts, interpret language, and use information to
        draw conclusions. Linguistic analysis helps state the distinctions a task is meant to test;
        behavioural experiments ask how people respond to them. Cognitive modelling is a direction
        I want to develop further, to turn competing explanations into predictions that can be
        compared with data.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        My <a href="/research/learnability">master’s thesis</a> asks how preschool children’s understanding
        of modal language relates to their reasoning about possibilities. It is a human developmental
        study being planned alongside an ongoing project at the Max Planck Institute for Human
        Cognitive and Brain Sciences. Language comprehension and task demands matter here: a child’s
        response does not, by itself, settle what concepts they possess.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Understanding artificial systems</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Language models are also research objects in their own right. I want to understand how
        they process information and make decisions. Mechanistic interpretability is one way to
        study this: intervening on internal representations can help test explanations of a model’s
        behaviour. Explaining existing abilities is a central motivation for my work.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        In my project on <a href="/research/latent-control-states">moral framing and dilemma choices</a>,
        I investigate how moral language changes a model’s decisions and the internal processes
        involved. The question includes how it weighs outcomes, how it responds to the answer format,
        and how far controlled interventions can distinguish those explanations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rp-sec">
      <h2 class="rp-h2">What connects the work</h2>
      <p class="rp-p">
        Across these projects, I ask how meaning enters a system’s reasoning and what evidence
        supports an explanation of its behaviour. Human and model studies can inform each other
        without assuming that they use the same mechanisms. Similar answers can arise from different
        processes, and an intervention in a model does not establish a mechanism in people.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-p">
        I aim to make the alternatives explicit, design comparisons that distinguish them, and keep
        claims tied to what a task measures. My writing in semantics and pragmatics, including
        <a href="/research/typicality-in-referent-choice">typicality in referent choice</a>, develops
        the conceptual distinctions behind those comparisons.
      </p>
    </section>
    <p class="rp-foot"><a href="/research">Explore research, writing, and course projects →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

